aptPOS Android Architecture Audit

1. Project Overview

The objective is to create a complete Android version of aptPOS while preserving the existing React user interface and POS functionality.

The existing Windows/Electron application on the "main" branch must remain unchanged.

All Android development must happen in the "android-port" branch.

2. Existing Technology Stack

The current project uses:

- React 19
- TypeScript
- Vite
- Electron 33
- Express.js
- SQLite using sql.js
- Tailwind CSS

3. Existing Architecture

Frontend

- Main entry: "src/main.tsx"
- Main application: "src/App.tsx"
- POS interface components: "src/components/"
- Styling: Tailwind CSS

Backend

- Main backend: "server.ts"
- Database operations: "server/db/"
- API communication: HTTP endpoints under "/api/*"

Desktop Integration

The Electron application currently handles:

- Application lifecycle
- File system access
- Printing
- Backup and restore
- Windows-specific dialogs
- Application updates
- IPC communication

These Electron-specific features cannot be assumed to work directly on Android.

4. Proposed Android Architecture

Frontend

Continue using the existing React interface.

Use Capacitor to package the React application as a native Android application.

Database

Implement native Android SQLite storage.

Requirements:

- Fully offline operation
- Persistent local data
- Reliable invoice numbering
- Transaction-safe sales operations
- Product and stock management
- Database backup and restore

Backend/API

Create an Android-compatible service layer.

The existing Express backend and database operations must be migrated or adapted rather than assuming the desktop server can run unchanged on Android.

Native Android Integration

Replace Electron APIs with Android-compatible implementations.

Required integrations:

- Local file access
- Database storage
- Application lifecycle
- Backup and restore
- Barcode scanning
- Thermal printing

5. Thermal Printer Requirements

Support:

- Bluetooth ESC/POS printers
- USB ESC/POS printers
- 58mm receipt paper
- 80mm receipt paper
- English text
- Arabic text
- QR codes
- Barcodes
- Automatic receipt printing

Printer functionality must be tested on real Android devices and compatible printers.

6. Features to Preserve

The Android version should retain the existing features wherever technically possible:

- User login and authentication
- POS register
- Product management
- Barcode scanning
- Sales and payments
- Sales history
- Purchases
- Inventory management
- Customers
- Vendors
- Sales returns
- Cashbook
- Reports
- Store settings
- Receipt customization
- Database backup and restore

7. Development Milestones

- [ ] Complete frontend and backend API audit.
- [ ] Audit all Electron API dependencies.
- [ ] Define shared platform interfaces.
- [ ] Create Capacitor Android project.
- [ ] Implement native SQLite integration.
- [ ] Migrate existing database operations.
- [ ] Implement offline POS operations.
- [ ] Implement Bluetooth printing.
- [ ] Implement USB printing.
- [ ] Implement Android backup and restore.
- [ ] Configure GitHub Actions.
- [ ] Generate Android APK.
- [ ] Test all major POS functions.

8. GitHub Build Requirements

Use GitHub Actions for cloud-based Android builds.

Required outputs:

- Debug APK
- Release APK
- Build logs
- Downloadable APK artifacts

Android signing credentials must be stored securely using GitHub repository secrets.

9. Important Development Rules

1. Never modify the existing "main" branch.
2. Keep all Android changes in "android-port".
3. Preserve the existing POS design.
4. Do not remove existing POS features without a technical reason.
5. Do not use a simple WebView wrapper as the final Android implementation.
6. Ensure offline database operations work correctly.
7. Prevent duplicate invoice numbers.
8. Protect existing business data.
9. Test printing on physical hardware.
10. Do not declare the Android application production-ready until the core features have been tested.

10. Final Objective

Deliver a complete, installable Android APK of aptPOS with offline SQLite database support, existing POS functionality, Bluetooth and USB thermal printer support, and reliable backup and restore.

The Android application must operate independently of the Windows Electron application while sharing the existing business logic and design wherever possible.